d those forms into  symbols and the symbols and information  lines are everywhere and you will  manipulate them through lighting angles  and your set design use them to point us  at things that are important use them to  focus our attention there's a reason why  the edge light is one of the most  powerful lighting tricks to make an  image look pretty and captivating  contrast is the next tool you have in  your arsenal how do you separate  foreground and background how do you  make something seem bright or dark  how do you perceive form and shape well  the answer lies in contrast for you to  know that something is bright you need  to see something that's dark as a point  of reference if you want to bring  attention to an element or an idea in  your frame adding something there to  contrast against it will do the job  color is the final tool we have in our  arsenal color brings to mind certain  emotions and they can highlight our  attention through contrast as well now  oftentimes movies that we think are  colorful are very frequently just  monochromatic but the whole image is  tinted a certain color to inspire an  emotion keep in mind that while you can  get as stylized as you want with your  colors people often prefer to see skin  tones as they naturally are which is a  basic orangish hue hey guys it's me  again back for another ad break uh this  time i want to tell you about the green  light feature on quarterdigital.com it  allows you to take your subscription  dollars and put them into projects you  want to see us make we already did phase  one that's what functional filmmaking is  all about we've got crew cuts we've got  son of a dungeon local zero we also just  introduced phase two of green light  which has a bunch of new awesome  projects that we want to get in  production now including corridor live  corridor forums new bones for peter  jake's dream project ah don't do  my dream project  you just he just ruined my dream project  now that we've defined what makes a good  image how do we get that from any camera  well by understanding the strengths of  our particular camera a good craftsman  lets their tools influence their work so  how do we get your camera to influence  your image well first put the camera in  your hands even if it's just a phone  think back to how we define what makes  an emotionally compelling image  information movement and visual beauty  and the different ways we can achieve  that so what aspects of an emotionally  compelling image can your camera easily  capture bone it's easy to get unique  angles with you have strength through  composition a gopro is easy to get  movement with so you have strength and  movement dslr gives you great separation  through focus so you have strength and  contrast the red gives you a very high  fidelity image so you have strength  through lighting so just examples but  every camera opens up certain  opportunities to truly get the best from  your camera you need to think about what  it is that makes your camera unique and  then creatively integrate that strength  into your filmmaking every camera is  associated with a vibe or a genre phones  are associated with viral videos dslrs  are associated with independent  filmmaking and vlogging gopros are  associated with action sports if you're  stuck using a certain camera consider  tweaking your story to play towards the  genre your camera is associated with if  i had to shoot a zombie movie on the  gopro  well i would direct it with a frenetic  energy that complements the action  sports genre that gopros are known for  whereas if i had to shoot down a red i  would make this piece much more moodier  and darker and try to go for something  tension-filled  [Music]  now if you tried to shoot the moody and  dark version of the zombie movie on a  gopro  well your camera strengths would not  really support the elements you need to  capture that appropriate emotion this  isn't to say it's impossible to shoot  something cinematic on