  think a neutral white point is in order  to correctly represent colors relative  to each other many times your camera  guesses wrong why is everybody blue  yuck enemy number seven  unintentional camera shake imagine  trying to look at a painting in a museum  but the pain just randomly shifts a few  feet in every direction yuck so more  expensive cameras have more tolerance  when it comes to these pesky problems  but they don't eliminate them that's  where your skillful hand as an artist  comes in now in order to clarify how  this all works i want to create an  analogy  here we have a grid of buckets  each bucket is a photosite on your  sensor and water is going to represent  our light  this is my light  when a water droplet lands in a bucket  it's like a photon hitting your camera  sensor now here's where the universe  conspires against us and our cameras the  universe is filled with random particles  of energy flying around  sorry  these energy particles affect your  camera's sensor to represent this we are  going to have it lightly rain over these  buckets  so now these buckets are out in the rain  slowly filling up with water even though  we haven't even shined any light on them  yet so now i'll turn on my light which  is represented by this hose and shine it  on the camera sensors  [Applause]  now we can measure the amount of water  in the buckets to see how much light has  hit them but we need to account for the  rain looking at the buckets before we  shine a light at them we can see that  there is about an inch of rain water so  anything over that is the light that  we've added  this is a representation of  signal-to-noise ratio our hose of light  is the signal and the rain is the noise  if a bucket is filled up with seven  inches of water and the rain averaged  one inch then it's easy to get a  confident measurement of the amount of  light the bucket actually absorbed but  if only a few drops of our water ended  up in the bucket well we're gonna have a  really hard time telling the difference  between our signal from the hose and the  noise which is the rain so the same  thing applies to real cameras there's  only a little bit of light in the room  your camera is going to have a very hard  time distinguishing between what's your  signal the light and what's the random  energy noise therefore if you want to  get a good image with your camera you  need to give your scene enough light  that you are above the signal-to-noise  ratio better cameras have better  signal-to-noise ratios and require less  light to get a clear image  experiment with your camera do some  tests and learn what its signal to noise  capabilities are let that inform how  much light you need for your scene now  let's say you decide to make sure you  have no issues with flight so you decide  to blast your scene with light  [Music]  [Applause]  i've i lit your scene great now you've  created another problem your buckets are  full you can't tell if you dumped one  gallon or 10 gallons of water on your  buckets because at a certain point your  bucket can't fill up anymore  this is an analogy for highlight  clipping also referred to as blowing out  your whites now this is one of your  greatest enemies when it comes to  shooting digitally and it's a challenge  because you need to bring enough light  into your scene to rise above that noise  floor but not so much light as to fill  up your bucket all the way and clip the  brightest parts of your image the amount  of water that we can measure between our  noise floor and when the bucket is full  is referred to as our dynamic range if  we replace our buckets with tiny little  cups we certainly find ourselves with a  very difficult challenge  [Applause]  i broke my camera the cups fill up  almost instantly if we shine light at  them but too little light and we can't  tell the difference from noise so cheap  cameras basically have the equivalent of  cups whereas expensive cameras have the  equivalent of buckets taller buckets  have more dynamic range skinny buckets  have better signal-to-noise ratios the  taller and skinnier the bucket the  better and often more expensive the  sensor is in order to get the best image  out of your sensor you need to control  the amount of light in your scene to  keep it within your dynamic range  this singular concept when mastered will  be one of the most powerful skills you  can have to get a great image out of  your camera so we have yet to talk about  any adjustments you can do to your image  on the computer you know after you've  picked up any camera you've shot your  video you're almost ready to export it  and share with the world  let's just talk about color grading for  a minute it's an extremely powerful tool  to have in your pocket and one of the  key ingredients to getting a great  compelling image the problem is  there are hundreds of different color  profiles for hundreds of different  cameras and many different color  sciences for the countless different  displays and monitors out there  so in color grading wouldn't it be nice  if there was just a single universal  method that you could use as a guideline  to dial in your image well there is it's  called the zone system and it was  invented by the renowned landscape  photographer ansel adams this is the  method that i use to guide my color  grading no matter what camera i'm using  or what screen i'm working on thankfully  the zone system is super simple and easy  to use here's how it works imagine a  gradient from black to white now divide  that gradient into 11 steps with black  being your first step also known as zone  zero and then each subsequent brighter  step increasing evenly until you reach  white on your 11th step aka zone 10. so  now you have 11 zones that represent  different tonality ranges zone 0 is pure  black zone 5 is a middle gray zone 10 is  pure white no matter what display you're  using or what color science you're using  you can imagine any image on any scre